comments.body:
If you are ok with doing a copy to host then the following is a workaround 

```
def torch2jax(x):
   return jax.device_put(x.numpy())
```

Note that this would copy to host and then copy to device. But it should handle the cases where `a` comes from a torch pipeline with slicing/transpositions which can produce strides that XLA minor_to_major doesn't handle

Thanks for the quick responses! The proposed workaround works for me. 

Another option I found to work in my example is to do a .contiguous() copy on the torch side to avoid the host-roundtrip
```
def torch2jax(x):
    return jax.dlpack.from_dlpack(torch.utils.dlpack.to_dlpack(x.contiguous()))
```

@qthequartermasterman try:
```
def torch2jax(x_torch: torch.Tensor):
    return jax.dlpack.from_dlpack(x_torch)
```
(it works here with jax 0.4.23, torch 2.1.2).

Notice that using `to_dlpack` is unnecessary and it is not a part of dlpack protocol

